"A hand is not only for holding. Your fingertips have more nerve endings packed into them than almost any other part of your body, so they can feel a single hair or read the dots of braille. Most people use one hand more than the other: about nine people in ten are right-handed. Left-handed or right-handed, your hands learn from practice. Now tape down your thumb and try the thumb challenge below the model.": "3d8acaaffb04",
"A tendon joins a muscle to a bone.": "0ad81c44371f",
"He gave me a thumbs-up.": "313f893e2446",
"Hold the coin in your palm.": "9c0328fb8c2a",
"How many bones are in one hand, and where are they?": "b693706e12ff",
"I can feel the bumps with my fingertips.": "13ba02ea2954",
"Keep a firm grip on the rope.": "bf56a839b756",
"Make a fist, and then open your hand.": "cbe0ef414d9e",
"Now the surprise. There are no muscles in your fingers. The muscles that bend and straighten them are in your forearm, and they are joined to your finger bones by long, strong cords called tendons. When a forearm muscle gets shorter, it pulls its tendon, and the tendon pulls the finger, like the string of a puppet. That is why your fingers can be thin and quick: the heavy engines are kept somewhere else. The palm does have some small muscles of its own, for spreading the fingers and for moving the thumb.": "0e2e4ae79ff8",
"Pinch the clay between your thumb and finger.": "da3d6bd373b7",
"She wears a watch on her left wrist.": "6dfddef41b0c",
"Start with the bones. One hand has 27 of them: 8 small bones in the wrist, 5 in the palm, and 14 in the fingers. Each finger has three bones, but the thumb has only two. With so many bones and joints, your hand can wrap around a ball, fit into a glove, or flatten out to clap.": "718a137d1600",
"The thumb is the star. At its base is a joint shaped like a saddle, which lets the thumb swing across the palm. It can touch the tip of every other finger. This is called an opposable thumb. Try it now: thumb to index finger, middle finger, ring finger, and little finger. Without that move, you could not hold a pencil, turn a key, or button a shirt.": "ebb5920445cb",
"What is the difference between a power grip and a precision grip?": "532f66dcc5cb",
"Where are the muscles that bend your fingers, and how do they reach the fingers?": "4db43e6af957",
"Wiggle your fingers and watch the back of your hand. Do you see thin cords moving under the skin? Now hold your forearm with your other hand and wiggle again. You can feel muscles working near your elbow, far away from your fingers. Your hand is one of the cleverest tools in nature, and it works a little like a puppet.": "2c64527eecac",
"Wiggle your fingers and watch the back of your hand. Do you see thin cords moving under the skin? Now hold your forearm with your other hand and wiggle again. You can feel muscles working near your elbow, far away from your fingers. Your hand is one of the cleverest tools in nature, and it works a little like a puppet. Start with the bones. One hand has 27 of them: 8 small bones in the wrist, 5 in the palm, and 14 in the fingers. Each finger has three bones, but the thumb has only two. With so many bones and joints, your hand can wrap around a ball, fit into a glove, or flatten out to clap. Now the surprise. There are no muscles in your fingers. The muscles that bend and straighten them are in your forearm, and they are joined to your finger bones by long, strong cords called tendons. When a forearm muscle gets shorter, it pulls its tendon, and the tendon pulls the finger, like the string of a puppet. That is why your fingers can be thin and quick: the heavy engines are kept somewhere else. The palm does have some small muscles of its own, for spreading the fingers and for moving the thumb. The thumb is the star. At its base is a joint shaped like a saddle, which lets the thumb swing across the palm. It can touch the tip of every other finger. This is called an opposable thumb. Try it now: thumb to index finger, middle finger, ring finger, and little finger. Without that move, you could not hold a pencil, turn a key, or button a shirt. Your hand has two main ways to hold things. In a power grip, all the fingers wrap around something and squeeze, as when you hold a bat or carry a bucket. In a precision grip, the thumb presses against the fingertips, as when you pick up a coin or thread a needle. Your hand switches between the two without your having to think about it. A hand is not only for holding. Your fingertips have more nerve endings packed into them than almost any other part of your body, so they can feel a single hair or read the dots of braille. Most people